			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1772836147181,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Below are some constructs when using both Kafka and Schema Registry:",
			"rawText": "Below are some constructs when using both Kafka and Schema Registry:",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Below are some constructs when using both Kafka and Schema Registry:",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25
		},
		{
			"id": "vVuzb92q",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -2901.0722767679445,
			"y": 1713.1710666369108,
			"width": 790.8194580078125,
			"height": 125,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "aI",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1931995732,
			"version": 208,
			"versionNonce": 1229611949,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1772836147181,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Message: a data item that is made up of a key (optional) and value\nTopic: a collection of messages, where ordering is maintained for those messages\nwith the same key (via underlying partitions)\nSchema (or event type): a description of how data should be structured\nSubject: a named, ordered history of schema versions",
			"rawText": "Message: a data item that is made up of a key (optional) and value\nTopic: a collection of messages, where ordering is maintained for those messages with the same key (via underlying partitions)\nSchema (or event type): a description of how data should be structured\nSubject: a named, ordered history of schema versions",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "Message: a data item that is made up of a key (optional) and value\nTopic: a collection of messages, where ordering is maintained for those messages with the same key (via underlying partitions)\nSchema (or event type): a description of how data should be structured\nSubject: a named, ordered history of schema versions",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25
		},
		{
			"id": "os1lNm9b",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -2895.0727122463213,
			"y": 1883.5663390805476,
			"width": 769.5394897460938,
			"height": 425,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "aL",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1924667244,
			"version": 486,
			"versionNonce": 1929078285,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1772836147181,
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "The following are some constraints that are maintained when using both Kafka\nand Schema Registry:\n\nSchema-message constraints: A schema constrains the structure of the\nmessage. The key and value are typically associated with different schemas.\nThe association between a schema and the key or value is embedded in the\nserialized form of the key or value.\n\nSubject-schema constraints: A subject constrains the ordered history of\nschema versions, also known as the evolution of the schema. This constraint\nis called a compatibility level. The compatibility level is stored in Schema\nRegistry along with the history of schema versions.\n\nSubject-topic constraints: When using the default TopicNameStrategy, a\nsubject can constrain the collection of messages in a topic. The association\nbetween the subject and the topic is by convention, where the subject name is\n{topic}-key for the message key and {topic}-value for the message value.",
			"rawText": "The following are some constraints that are maintained when using both Kafka and Schema Registry:\n\nSchema-message constraints: A schema constrains the structure of the message. The key and value are typically associated with different schemas. The association between a schema and the key or value is embedded in the serialized form of the key or value.\n\nSubject-schema constraints: A subject constrains the ordered history of schema versions, also known as the evolution of the schema. This constraint is called a compatibility level. The compatibility level is stored in Schema Registry along with the history of schema versions.\n\nSubject-topic constraints: When using the default TopicNameStrategy, a subject can constrain the collection of messages in a topic. The association between the subject and the topic is by convention, where the subject name is {topic}-key for the message key and {topic}-value for the message value.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 5,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "The following are some constraints that are maintained when using both Kafka and Schema Registry:\n\nSchema-message constraints: A schema constrains the structure of the message. The key and value are typically associated with different schemas. The association between a schema and the key or value is embedded in the serialized form of the key or value.\n\nSubject-schema constraints: A subject constrains the ordered history of schema versions, also known as the evolution of the schema. This constraint is called a compatibility level. The compatibility level is stored in Schema Registry along with the history of schema versions.\n\nSubject-topic constraints: When using the default TopicNameStrategy, a subject can constrain the collection of messages in a topic. The association between the subject and the topic is by convention, where the subject name is {topic}-key for the message key and {topic}-value for the message value.",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25
		},
